Find $w.$

answer:
\begin{align*}
w & = cz^{1 / 4} \\
& = \left ( 2 - 3i \right ) \left( \sqrt[4]{\frac{2 \pm 3i \sqrt{2}}{8}}\right )^{1 / 4} \\
& = \sqrt{\frac{2 \pm 3i \sqrt{2}}{8}}.
\end{align*}

By the triangular inequality, we have $w \in \left[ -\sqrt{\frac{3}{2}}, \sqrt{\frac{3}{2}} \right].$  For example, if we take $z$ to be $12 - 9i,$ then we find that $c = 3 - 3i$ is the vector from the origin to the center of the circle.  The intersection of the lines $y = \sqrt{\frac{12}{9}},$ $y = \sqrt{\frac{9}{12}}$ and $y = \sqrt{\frac{3}{2}}$ is $\frac{2\sqrt{6}}{9} - \frac{3}{9}i,$ which satisfies $z = 12 - 9i = 3 - 3i \left( \frac{2\sqrt{6}}{9} - \frac{3}{9}i \right).$  Thus $w$ is the square root of $z,$ and \[w^2 = 12 + 9i = 3 - 3i.\] Therefore $w = \boxed{3 - 3i}.$
